=== (7) IS THE SHAM FAILURE A HALO? — a source-severity artifact predicts the four norm x sanction correlations to be ALIKE ===
  the four: ['+0.895', '+0.167', '+0.024', '+0.618'] · spread **0.871** ⇒ **NOT uniform: a halo cannot produce this**

====================================================================================================
**D is REFUTED by its own test, and the sham failure is behaviour-specific.**
  A source-severity halo predicts the four norm x sanction correlations to be ALIKE. Measured, they span **0.871**: ['+0.895', '+0.167', '+0.024', '+0.618']. `premarital norm x premarital sanction` **+0.895** against `premarital norm x extramarital sanction` **+0.024** — the same two columns, one coder, one ethnography, and a gap of 0.871. **A halo cannot do that.**
  What the sham DOES establish is narrower and it is the finding: `extramarital norm x PREMARITAL sanction` is **+0.618** while `extramarital norm x its OWN sanction` is **+0.167** ⇒ **premarital sanction sits on a general restrictiveness dimension; the punishment for adultery does not sit on it at all.**
  ⇒ **One sentence about people: how much a society says a sexual act is wrong tells you almost exactly what it will do to an unmarried woman who does it (rho +0.895, residual 0.20) and tells you essentially nothing about what it will do to a married one (rho +0.167, residual 0.97). Condemnation and punishment are the same axis for one act and two axes for the other — so 'how many rifts' is not a property of a society, it is a property of the act.**

